      "dom_id": "question-16551",
      "name": null,
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/foundations/14169858/assets/0faaa905476ef6b6224a4b6b24ceb1adde4b14680507a7c6289885dce1bdd997.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the ellipse given above?",
      "worked_solution": "Remember that the general equation of an ellipse centered at $(h,k)$ with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{(x-h)^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{(y-k)^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nHere, the ellipse is centered at $(3,-1),$ the horizontal radius has length $a=3,$ and the vertical radius has length $b=5.$ Therefore, the equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x-h)^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{(y-k)^{2}}{{b}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x-3)^{2}}{{3}^{2}}+\\frac{(y-(-1))^{2}}{{5}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x-3)^{2}}{9}+\\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{25} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
